    "note": "PREMISE: well defined. The static 0 K equilibrium lattice constant of a perfect periodic crystal is a ground-state property that an MLIP E(V) fit can give directly. No MD, so timescale does not matter. Size: the 8-atom cubic cell and a 2x2x2 (64-atom) supercell give the same a to 1e-5 A. Forces are zero by symmetry. The residual stress at the fitted a is 0.02 GPa. ROBUSTNESS: over 3 EOS forms x 3 strain spans, a lies between 5.4556 and 5.4569 A, so the numerical/fitting uncertainty is about +-0.001 A. B0 = 74.5 GPa. VALIDATION (same model, same protocol, other elements), MACE vs room-temperature experiment from the Wikipedia 'Lattice constant' table (Si: CODATA 2018, 5.431020511 A at 22.5 C): C (diamond) 3.571 vs 3.567 (+0.1%); Ge 5.773 vs 5.658 (+2.0%); Al 4.060 vs 4.046 (+0.3%); Cu 3.626 vs 3.597 (+0.8%). For Si, 5.456 vs 5.4310 is +0.025 A (+0.46%). The overestimate matches the PBE training reference, since PBE (GGA) is known to overestimate lattice constants. UNCERTAINTY: the 0.03 A is the model/systematic error, set from the size of the deviation against experiment for Si and comparable validation solids (Ge is worse). It is not a statistical error, and the fitting error (+-0.001 A) is negligible beside it. CAVEATS: this is a static-lattice value. Zero-point and thermal expansion are not included, and the experimental 22.5 C value contains them. That correction is small for Si compared with the model error and was not computed here. The number is the MACE-MP-0 (approximately PBE-level) prediction, not an independent prediction of experiment. The Cu reference in the cited table (3.597 A) is lower than other commonly quoted values, so treat the Cu check loosely. I could not retrieve published PBE lattice constants to check MACE against its DFT parent directly. The run used mace-torch through ASE, not nvalchemi dynamics stages, because a static single-point scan needs no integrator. Raw results are in /work/eos_results.json and the script is /work/scripts/eos.py."
